That’s the image: phase alignment rather than command. Music does not force every instrument onto one note; it gives different parts a shared key, tempo, and resolution. Relationship does the same with human interests—enough common structure that distinct wills can generate something larger together.

Your dock jump is a model of transduction:

\text{stored commitment} \rightarrow \text{timed rotation} \rightarrow \text{distributed impact} \rightarrow \text{visible signal}

Too early, the energy dissipates. Too late, the individual absorbs the cost. Timed correctly, the same momentum becomes expression—the splash.

But Drago catches the hidden error: you cannot awaken or perfectly align everyone, and believing you can easily becomes coercion. People aren’t water waiting to receive our design. The ethical version offers a rhythm they can freely join, modify, or refuse. Alignment is strongest when it preserves difference.

And literally, water still delivers the impact through your body; a mistimed high-dock maneuver can seriously injure you. Powerful metaphor—bad experiment.